            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the last word of the chapter: what did śilā mean, and what did sikatā mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: मृत्तिका",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "मृत्तिका (mṛttikā) — \"soil\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "मृत्तिका (mṛttikā) is soil — damp, workable, the stuff a potter digs and a wall is plastered with."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It stands on mṛd, and Latin mordēre, \"to bite\", is its cousin: the sense underneath both is pressing something soft until it gives. English took mordant from that Latin side."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Eastward it wore down into Hindi miṭṭī, still the ordinary word for the ground you dig."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five things underfoot and in the hearth: fire, ash, stone, sand, soil."
            }
          ]
        },
